<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Zhou</h1><p class="st-meta">557 CE – 581 CE · capital Chang'an</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Yuwen family's dynasty in north-western China, which conquered Northern Qi and built the military system that the Sui and Tang inherited.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a>, the strongman of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-wei">Western Wei</a>, had built a powerful army based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. After his death in 556 his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-hu">Yuwen Hu</a> forced the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> emperor to abdicate in 557 in favour of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yuwen-xianbei">Yuwen</a> Tai's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-jue">Yuwen Jue</a>, who took the archaic title Heavenly King of Zhou.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime named its offices after the classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou">Rites of Zhou</a> to give itself ancient legitimacy, and relied on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fubing-system">fubing</a> militia, which bound <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and Han soldiers to the state through land grants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-hu">Yuwen Hu</a> dominated the court until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> killed him in 572. Emperor Wu then centralised power, banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Daoism</a> in 574 to seize monastic wealth and manpower, and conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> in 577.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> died in 578 and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuan-of-northern-zhou">Emperor Xuan</a> ruled erratically. When the child Emperor Jing succeeded, the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Yang Jian</a>, father of Emperor Xuan's empress, crushed opposition and took the throne in 581 as founder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 557 CE – 581 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:70.79%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>557 CE – 581 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chang'an</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yuwen Jue (Emperor Xiaomin), Yuwen Yong (Emperor Wu), Yuwen Yun (Emperor Xuan)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism, Daoism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Xianbei</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zhou (Yuwen clan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=569&amp;focus=northern-zhou" aria-label="Northern Zhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7449 935 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Zhou lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7769 1191L7769 1191M7769 1191L7879 1125M7769 1191L7769 1191M7769 1191L6820 913M7769 1191L7920 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7879 1125h0M7769 1191h0M6820 913h0M7920 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chang'an. <a href="/atlas?year=569&amp;focus=northern-zhou">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
